<Layout
  title="Thread - Soft Goods - DES4800"
  description="Gütermann Mara, Mara rPET, and Tera thread for product design soft-goods work."
>
  <article class="post-detail">
    <header class="post-header">
      <a href="/soft-goods/" class="back-link">← Back to Soft Goods</a>
      <h1>Thread</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="post-content">
      <p>
        In this course we will primarily use the <a href="https://guetermann.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gütermann</a> brand's thread called
        <strong> Mara</strong>. Gütermann is a reliable thread with
        good weight options, consistent color ranges, and strengths suited to both fine garment seams and heavy-duty product work.</p>
        <p>
        Matching thread type and weight to fabric and use is as important as choosing the fabric itself. A weak or wrong-size thread it will fail, look funky (in a bad way), or both. So let's dive into those details.
      </p>

      <h2 id="thread-size-numbers">Thread size numbers are confusing</h2>
      <p>
        The first thing to know is that there are many different systems for thread size numbers. The two we will be using in this course are the Gutermann system and the Tex (often shown as T) system. But know that there are many other systems out there. Here's a list of some of the most common systems:
        <ul>
          <li>Gutermann system (we will be using this one)</li>
          <li>Tex system (often shown as T)</li>
          <li>Denier system (often shown as Td or d)</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.amann-mettler.com/en/products/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mettler thread numbering system</a></li>
          <li>and more</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        The Gutermann system is the system we will be using most frequently in this course.
      </p>
      <h3 id="gutermann-thread-tickets">Gütermann thread sizing (aka "tickets")</h3>
      <figure class="content-figure">
        <img
          src="/images/soft-goods/gutermann-mara-70.jpg"
          alt="Gütermann Mara thread spool with ticket number 70 shown on the yellow end label"
          width="800"
          height="533"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Example spool: The word "Mara" is followed by the ticket number <strong>70</strong> on the cone end. 
          You can see the "7" and a bit of the "0" on the yellow end in this photo. This is the Mara weight, not the Tex number.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Gütermann’s numbers are their own system (commercial systems are often called "tickets") may feel backwards. In the commercial/ticket system
        used for Gütermann’s threads, <strong>the bigger the number, the finer (thinner) the thread</strong>.</p>
        <p>
          For example, Mara 220 is much thinner than Mara 30. Bigger number, thinner thread.
        </p>
        <p>
          Why the word "ticket?" This number used to be printed on a physical paper ticket attached to the spool. Now it is printed on the cone end.
        </p>
      <h3 id="tex-numbers">Tex numbers</h3>
      <p>
        Other threads use the <strong>Tex</strong> system. Tex is kind of the <em>metric system for thread weight</em> and it is an international standard.</p>
        <p>A <em>higher</em> Tex number means a <em>thicker</em> thread.</p>
        <p>Tex = weight in grams of thread per 1,000 meters. 
          <p>Here's a couple of Tex examples:<ul>
            <li>Let's say we have a thread that weighs <strong>12 grams</strong> per 1,000 meters. This means the Tex number is <strong>12.</strong></li>
            <li>If the thread weighs <strong>24 grams</strong> per 1,000 meters, the Tex number is <strong>24.</strong></li></ul></p>
            <p>
              The tex number goes up as the thread strand gets heavier.</p>
              </p>
        <p>We will mostly stick to Gutermann's ticket number system for this course as we will be using Gütermann’s Mara and Tera in ticket numbers 100 and 70 in this course.</p>
        <p>Here's a little table to help you understand the relationship between the two systems.</p>
